<figcaption>A weekend of portability stories: models moving between chips, clouds and desks.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Amazon reportedly nears a deal for Decart</h2>
<p>Amazon is in advanced talks to buy Decart, a startup whose software helps AI models run across different chips including AWS's own Trainium, in a deal reported at about $7.5 billion that could be announced soon, according to reporting citing the Financial Times<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Nothing has been announced, and reports note Decart was valued at $4 billion earlier in the year<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> the strategic point is software that makes non-Nvidia chips easier to use. If it works, AWS customers get more realistic alternatives for inference, and more price competition. Do not plan around a rumour, but do keep your serving stack portable: standard model formats, a serving layer you control and evaluation sets that let you compare accelerators on your own workloads. See <a href="/insights/llm-inference-serving-vllm/">LLM inference serving</a> and <a href="/insights/ai-memory-squeeze-inference-architecture/">inference architecture under memory constraints</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Thinking Machines releases Inkling 2 Mini with open weights</h2>
<p>Thinking Machines Lab released Inkling 2 Mini under Apache 2.0, a looped mixture-of-experts model whose middle layers run several times per token, with a reported memory footprint of about 20 GB<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Coverage says it accepts text and images and is pitched to run on well-equipped laptops; benchmark claims come from the lab and independent testers and should be checked against your own tasks<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> capable open-weight models that run on a developer's machine change two things: private experimentation without sending data out, and cheaper fallbacks for routine tasks. Add it to your evaluation set alongside your current models, and record its lineage in your model inventory, as in <a href="/insights/model-provenance-open-weight-models/">model provenance</a>. Licence terms matter: check the acceptable use policy as well as the Apache licence.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. RTX Spark compact desktops go on sale</h2>
<p>Nvidia said compact desktops built on its RTX Spark platform are now available from ASUS, Dell and MSI, with machines from Acer, GIGABYTE, HP and a Microsoft developer box to follow<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The Next Web reports prices starting at $4,399, with configurations up to 128 GB of unified memory<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> machines like this make local model work practical for small teams: prototyping, fine-tuning small models and running evaluations without cloud queues or data leaving the building. They are not production infrastructure. Treat them as developer workstations, with disk encryption, device management and clear rules on which data may sit on them. See my notes on <a href="/insights/dgx-spark-ai-architect-bench/">an AI architect's desktop bench</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Nvidia investigates a possible partner incident after a ransomware claim</h2>
<p>Nvidia said it is investigating an incident that may have affected a third-party partner, after the Gentlemen ransomware group listed the company on its leak site, and that it has found no indication its own systems or networks were affected<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. The group had not published evidence at the time of reporting.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> "a partner may be affected" is now the most common shape of a large company's incident. If you share data with partners, know exactly what each one holds, set expectations for notification in contracts and be ready to answer customers before the facts are complete. See <a href="/insights/vendor-admin-access-rmm-breaches/">vendor access as perimeter</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Check how portable your model serving is across accelerators and clouds.</li>
<li>Add a capable small open-weight model to your evaluation set as a fallback candidate.</li>
<li>Set rules for local AI workstations: encryption, management and permitted data.</li>
<li>Review what data each partner holds and how quickly they must tell you about incidents.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> and <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. If any of these lands on your desk this week, <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
